The container image built from it (ghcr.io/kaperkunde/pdf-mcp-server)
bundles pymupdf and MuPDF, licensed under AGPL-3.0, together with this
project's Apache-2.0 code and the Debian packages of its base image
(poppler-utils is GPL-2.0/GPL-3.0, tesseract-ocr Apache-2.0). Publishing
the image is distributing a combined work that includes AGPL-3.0 software,
so:

  - Whoever distributes the image offers the complete corresponding source
    of its AGPL-3.0 and GPL parts: this repository at the image's tag,
    uv.lock (which names each Python package version and its PyPI hash),
    and the upstream sources of pymupdf/MuPDF and the Debian packages.
    This repository being public, with the tag the image was built from,
    is how Kaperkunde meets that for its own code and pins.
  - Anyone who modifies the image's AGPL-3.0 components and lets others
    use it over a network must offer those users the modified source
    (AGPL-3.0 section 13). Running the unmodified image for oneself,
    as a PCP owner does, triggers no such duty.
  - Using a commercial pymupdf licence from Artifex instead is the only way
    to distribute the image under terms other than AGPL-3.0 for that part.

